{"duration": 57.391, "exact_word_timing": true, "lines": [{"beat": 0, "text": "A Secretary of War begged Congress for camels.", "start": 0.08, "end": 2.464}, {"beat": 0, "text": "Jefferson Davis wanted them to haul supplies across the desert Southwest.", "start": 2.684, "end": 6.847}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Soldiers stared at the humped beasts arriving from the Mediterranean.", "start": 7.267, "end": 10.711}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Mules were the Army way.", "start": 10.931, "end": 12.284}, {"beat": 1, "text": "Camels? Nobody knew how to handle them.", "start": 12.504, "end": 14.615}, {"beat": 2, "text": "Major Wayne shipped 34 camels to Texas in 1856.", "start": 15.035, "end": 18.965}, {"beat": 2, "text": "A second voyage brought 41 more.", "start": 19.185, "end": 21.112}, {"beat": 2, "text": "The herd reached 70.", "start": 21.332, "end": 22.536}, {"beat": 3, "text": "In 1857, Edward Beale led 25 camels on a wagon road survey.", "start": 22.956, "end": 27.647}, {"beat": 3, "text": "Each camel carried 600 pounds.", "start": 27.867, "end": 29.807}, {"beat": 3, "text": "Beale said one camel beat four mules.", "start": 30.027, "end": 32.058}, {"beat": 4, "text": "You see, a mule needs water and rest.", "start": 32.478, "end": 34.522}, {"beat": 4, "text": "Camels drink once, then keep walking for days.", "start": 34.742, "end": 37.369}, {"beat": 5, "text": "In 1859, Lieutenant Hartz marched camels 85 miles with no water.", "start": 37.789, "end": 42.481}, {"beat": 5, "text": "They showed no thirst.", "start": 42.701, "end": 43.915}, {"beat": 5, "text": "Horses and mules arrived distressed; camels did not.", "start": 44.135, "end": 47.196}, {"beat": 6, "text": "The Army still said no.", "start": 47.616, "end": 48.88}, {"beat": 6, "text": "Then the Civil War hit.", "start": 49.1, "end": 50.366}, {"beat": 6, "text": "Camels were sold at auction in 1864.", "start": 50.586, "end": 53.25}, {"beat": 6, "text": "The last one was seen in Arizona in 1891.", "start": 53.47, "end": 56